  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Record warmth possible on Thursday.\n\nAn expansive surface ridge over the area will begin to drift \noff to our east today. While this occurs,return southerly flow \nwill develop on the western flank over the region which will aid\nin boosting temperatures back 'well' above normal, especially \non Thursday. With the expected warmth, some records may fall or \nbe tied, especially at Rochester (KROC) and in the Genesee \nValley due to downslope flow and dry soils (see Climate section \nbelow). Additionally, winds will strengthening and then become \nquite breezy (gusts to 35 mph) on Thursday, strongest winds will\nlikely be northeast of lake Erie. Given the cooler lake waters \nand expected SW flow, Max T's may just fall short of record \nterritory at KBUF/KIAG. Despite that, more gorgeous fall \nconditions will continue areawide today and Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief shot of light rain Thursday night. Dry \nweather then resumes and lasts through the weekend. \n\nA weak cold front nears the region Thursday which will bring a \nchance for some showers Thursday evening and night. In terms of \nprecipitation, amounts look very meager with only a few hundredths \nof an inch at best possible across Western and Central NY. Given the \nstreak of dry weather, some fire weather concerns will remain due to \nthe dryness of fuels and leaf litter. Luckily, winds and RH levels \nwon't reach close to critical levels through the weekend. See Fire \nweather section below for more details. High pressure return over \nthe Great Lakes Friday and then sticks around through the weekend.",
